// Barber license — a state-issued credential, and one of the most fragmented in the catalogue.
//
// Read src/data/tracks/CONTRACT.md before editing. The sourcing rule is the product:
// every field below traces to a state board site, a state statute, or an official
// testing-vendor page, and 'unconfirmed' means no primary source published it.
//
// Two structural things make barbering harder to pin down than most licences:
//   1. WHO REGULATES IT VARIES. Some states keep a standalone Board of Barber Examiners
//      (Nebraska still runs its own examination). Others folded barbering into a combined
//      cosmetology board (New Jersey merged in 1984, Ohio, Alabama, Mississippi and Iowa
//      have all merged since). The board decides the exam, the hours and the scope.
//   2. THE TIERS VARY. Master barber vs. restricted or apprentice barber, a separate
//      straight-razor/shaving endorsement, and a barber-instructor licence all exist in
//      some states and not others.
//
// THREE CORRECTIONS TO THE WIDELY-COPIED 2024 HOUR TABLES:
//   * IOWA is no longer 2,100 hours. Iowa cut to 1,550 in 2023 and merged barbering and
//     cosmetology into ONE licence; a 2025 law added a supervised on-the-job route.
//   * NEBRASKA is not 2,100 either. Nebraska barber schools require a course of not less
//     than 1,800 hours, and the Board of Barber Examiners runs its own exam.
//   * CALIFORNIA and TEXAS both dropped to 1,000 hours. California did it in SB 803,
//     which also abolished the practical exam outright.

