  lessons: [
  {t:"Infection Control & Bloodborne Pathogens", d:"Disinfection, universal precautions, blood exposure procedure", len:"22 min",
   script:"Barbering is the one beauty trade where you routinely put a blade against skin, so infection control carries the heaviest weighting on the exam and the heaviest enforcement in the shop. Learn the ladder. Cleaning removes debris. Disinfection kills nearly all pathogens on a hard, non-porous surface. Sterilization kills everything including spores. Porous items go in the bin. Non-porous implements are cleaned first, then fully immersed in an E P A registered hospital disinfectant for the label's contact time. Universal precautions means you treat every client's blood as infectious. If you nick someone, you stop, glove up, apply pressure with a fresh gauze, clean and cover the wound, double-bag the contaminated waste, disinfect the implement or discard it, and log the incident."},
  {t:"Hair, Skin & Scalp Disorders", d:"What you may work around and what you must refuse", len:"20 min",
   script:"You need to tell a disorder from an infection before you touch anyone. Tinea capitis is ringworm of the scalp, a fungus, and it means refuse and refer. Impetigo and folliculitis are bacterial and also mean refusal. Head lice, or pediculosis capitis, means refusal. Now the ones you can work with. Dandruff, properly pityriasis, is a scaling condition you may service. Alopecia areata is patchy autoimmune hair loss and is not contagious. Androgenic alopecia is ordinary pattern balding. And know pseudofolliculitis barbae, the razor bumps common in clients with tightly curled hair, caused by a shaved hair curling back into the skin. The answer is never to shave closer. It is to shave with the grain and never below the skin surface."},
  {t:"Haircutting & Clipper Work", d:"Elevation, guards, tapers, fades and cross-checking", len:"30 min",
   script:"Cutting is geometry. Elevation is the angle you hold the hair away from the head, and it decides everything. Zero elevation gives you a blunt, one-length line with weight at the bottom. Ninety degrees gives you uniform layers. Anything higher removes weight and builds graduation. A taper is a gradual shortening as you move down the sides and the back. A fade is a taper taken all the way to skin or near it, described as low, mid, high, or bald depending on where the transition starts. Learn clipper-over-comb and shear-over-comb for blending where a guard would leave a line. And always cross-check. Comb the section in the opposite direction from the way you cut it, and the unevenness shows itself."},
  {t:"Shaving & Straight-Razor Safety", d:"Fourteen shaving areas, grain direction, blade rules", len:"26 min",
   script:"The straight-razor shave is the service that defines the licence and the one that hurts a client fastest. Prep with a hot towel to soften the beard and open the follicle, then lather to hold the moisture in. The first pass always goes with the grain. Only after that do you consider an across-the-grain pass, and against the grain is a finishing pass on a client whose skin tolerates it. Hold the skin taut, keep the blade at a shallow angle, and use the correct stroke, either free-hand or backhand, for the area you are working. Blade rule, and every board tests it. A conventional straight razor honed and reused between clients is prohibited in the great majority of states. You use a changeable-blade razor and a fresh blade for every client."},
  {t:"Chemical Services: Colour, Lightening & Texture", d:"pH, developers, relaxers, permanent waving, patch tests", len:"25 min",
   script:"Chemistry is where a barber licence and a barber's scope of practice often part company, so check your state. The science does not change. Permanent oxidative colour uses hydrogen peroxide developer to swell the cuticle, lighten existing pigment, and develop small dye precursors into large colour molecules trapped inside the cortex. Higher volume developer means more lift and more damage. Thio permanent waving breaks disulphide bonds with ammonium thioglycolate, then rebuilds them with an oxidising neutraliser. Hydroxide relaxers, the sodium hydroxide ones, break those bonds permanently and are not neutralised by thio. They are rinsed thoroughly and stopped with an acid-balanced neutralising shampoo. And a predisposition patch test goes on twenty-four to forty-eight hours before any aniline derivative tint."},
  {t:"Implements, Electricity & Shop Safety", d:"Clippers, shears, honing, electrical safety, chemical storage", len:"18 min",
   script:"Know your kit and the rules around it. Clipper guards run from a zero, leaving about a sixteenth of an inch, up through the numbers, each step adding roughly an eighth. A number two is a quarter of an inch. Shears are measured in inches and sized to your hand, and thinning or texturising shears remove bulk rather than length. A straight razor is honed on a stone and stropped on leather, but a changeable-blade razor is simply re-bladed. On electricity, know the difference between an amp, a volt and a watt, and know that a ground fault circuit interrupter outlet is what belongs near a shampoo bowl. Chemicals get stored in labelled original containers, and every product needs its safety data sheet on the premises."},
  {t:"State Barber Law, Hours & Shop Rules", d:"State-specific — updates automatically per state", len:"22 min", stateSpecific:true,
   script:null},
  ],

